903 0 obj <</Type /StructElem /S /H3 /T (TF-IDF: Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency is a statistical measure used to evaluate the importance of a word in a document relative to a collection of documents \(corpus\). It assigns higher weights to words that appear frequently in a document but infrequently across the corpus. ) /E (TF-IDF: Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency is a statistical measure used to evaluate the importance of a word in a document relative to a collection of documents \(corpus\). It assigns higher weights to words that appear frequently in a document but infrequently across the corpus. ) /Alt () /Pg 23 0 R /K [1 ] >> endobj
904 0 obj <</Type /StructElem /S /H3 /T (BM25: Best Match 25 is an advanced probabilistic model used to rank documents based on the query terms appearing in each document. It is part of the family of probabilistic information retrieval models and is considered an advancement over the classic TF-IDF model. The improvement that BM25 brings is that it adjusts for the length of the documents so that longer documents do not unfairly get higher scores. ) /E (BM25: Best Match 25 is an advanced probabilistic model used to rank documents based on the query terms appearing in each document. It is part of the family of probabilistic information retrieval models and is considered an advancement over the classic TF-IDF model. The improvement that BM25 brings is that it adjusts for the length of the documents so that longer documents do not unfairly get higher scores. ) /Alt () /Pg 23 0 R /K [2 ] >> endobj
905 0 obj <</Type /StructElem /S /H3 /T (Static Word Embeddings: Static embeddings like Word2Vec and GloVe represent words as dense vectors in a continuous vector space, capturing semantic relationships based on context. For instance, "king" - "man" + "woman" approximates "queen". These embeddings can capture nuances like similarity and analogy, which BoW, TF-IDF and BM25 miss.) /E (Static Word Embeddings: Static embeddings like Word2Vec and GloVe represent words as dense vectors in a continuous vector space, capturing semantic relationships based on context. For instance, "king" - "man" + "woman" approximates "queen". These embeddings can capture nuances like similarity and analogy, which BoW, TF-IDF and BM25 miss.) /Alt () /Pg 23 0 R /K [3 ] >> endobj
906 0 obj <</Type /StructElem /S /H3 /T (Contextual Embeddings: Contextual embeddings, generated by models such as BERT or OpenAI's text embeddings, produce high-dimensional, context-aware representations for queries and documents. These models, based on transformers, capture deep semantic meanings and relationships. For example, a query about "apple" will retrieve documents discussing apple the fruit or apple the technology company depending on the input query. ) /E (Contextual Embeddings: Contextual embeddings, generated by models such as BERT or OpenAI's text embeddings, produce high-dimensional, context-aware representations for queries and documents. These models, based on transformers, capture deep semantic meanings and relationships. For example, a query about "apple" will retrieve documents discussing apple the fruit or apple the technology company depending on the input query. ) /Alt () /Pg 23 0 R /K [4 ] >> endobj
